\\cos \\frac{\\pi i}{n}\\right\\}_{i=0}^{n},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(x = \\cos \\frac{\\pi i}{n}\\) are exactly the points where \\(|T_n(x)| = 1\\). They also showed that, if restrictions \\(|p(x)| \\le 1\\) are imposed at any other set of \\((n+1)\\) points in \\([-1,1]\\), then Markov bound (1.1) is no longer true.\n\nHere we consider the problem of estimating the norm \\(\\|p^{(k)}\\|\\) under restriction\n\n\\[\n|p(x)| \\le \\mu(x), \\qquad x \\in I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mu\\) is an arbitrary non-negative majorant, and \\(I\\) is either the whole interval \\([-1,1]\\), or a discrete set \\(\\delta\\) of \\(n+1\\) points in \\([-1,1]\\).\n\nSo, the problems are the following.\n\n* This author was supported by the National Science Fund of the Bulgarian Ministry of Education, Youth and Science under Grant DDVU-02/30.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDuffin and Schaeffer inequality revisited - Constructive Mathematical Analysis (https://doi.org/10.33205/cma.1632536)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Duffin and Schaeffer inequality revisited ... Schaeffer found a remarkable refinement of Markov inequality, showing that this extremal property of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ persists in the wider class of polynomials whose modulus is bounded by one at the extreme points of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$.Their result gives rise to the definition of DS-type inequalities, which are comparison-type theorems of the following nature: inequalities between the absolute values of two polynomials of degree not exceeding n n$n$ on a given set of n+1$n+1$ points in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$ induce inequalities between the C[−1,1]$C[-1,1]$-norms of their derivatives.\n\nResearch Article\n\n# Duffin and Schaeffer inequality revisited\n\nVolume: 8 Number: 2 June 15, 2025\n\n  * Geno Nikolov ^{*}\n\nConstructive Mathematical Analysis\n\nPDF Download Cite\n\n## Duffin and Schaeffer inequality revisited\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe classical Markov inequality asserts that the n n$n$-th Chebyshev polynomial T n(x)=cos n arccos x$T_n(x)=\\cos n\\arccos x$, x∈[−1,1]$x\\in [-1,1]$, has the largest C[−1,1]$C[-1,1]$-norm of its derivatives within the set of algebraic polynomials of degree at most n n$n$ whose absolute value in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$ does not exceed one. In 1941 R.J. Duffin and A.C. Schaeffer found a remarkable refinement of Markov inequality, showing that this extremal property of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ persists in the wider class of polynomials whose modulus is bounded by one at the extreme points of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$. Their result gives rise to the definition of DS-type inequalities, which are comparison-type theorems of the following nature: inequalities between the absolute values of two polynomials of degree not exceeding n n$n$ on a given set of n+1$n+1$ points in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$ induce inequalities between the C[−1,1]$C[-1,1]$-norms of their derivatives. Here we apply the approach from a 1992 paper of A. Shadrin to prove some DS-type inequalities where Jacobi polynomials are extremal. In particular, we obtain an extension of the result of Duffin and Schaeffer.\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Markov inequality\n  * Duffin–Schaeffer inequality\n  * Chebyshev polynomials\n  * Jacobi polynomials\n  * interlacing of zeros\n\n\n## Duffin and Schaeffer inequality revisited\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe classical Markov inequality asserts that the n n$n$-th Chebyshev polynomial T n(x)=cos n arccos x$T_n(x)=\\cos n\\arccos x$, x∈[−1,1]$x\\in [-1,1]$, has the largest C[−1,1]$C[-1,1]$-norm of its derivatives within the set of algebraic polynomials of degree at most n n$n$ whose absolute value in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$ does not exceed one. In 1941 R.J. Duffin and A.C. Schaeffer found a remarkable refinement of Markov inequality, showing that this extremal property of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ persists in the wider class of polynomials whose modulus is bounded by one at the extreme points of T n{T}_{n}$T_n$ in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$. Their result gives rise to the definition of DS-type inequalities, which are comparison-type theorems of the following nature: inequalities between the absolute values of two polynomials of degree not exceeding n n$n$ on a given set of n+1$n+1$ points in [−1,1]$[-1,1]$ induce inequalities between the C[−1,1]$C[-1,1]$-norms of their derivatives. Here we apply the approach from a 1992 paper of A. Shadrin to prove some DS-type inequalities where Jacobi polynomials are extremal. In particular, we obtain an extension of the result of Duffin and Schaeffer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPure Exploration for a Good Policy in Reinforcement Learning with Bandit Feedback (https://bittide.aicompass.dev/article/73ebbfcf-2a52-4d0b-93ad-1bf80742584c?cache=1&locale=en)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; We can prove Lemma[34](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.23182#Thmtheorem34)by applying the Markov Inequality\\. ...     Pr⁡\\(max1≤n≤N​∑t=1nXt\\>x\\)≤exp⁡\\(−x22​N​σ2\\)\\.\\\\displaystyle\\\\Pr\\\\left\\(\\\\max\\_\\{1\\\\leq n\\\\leq N\\}\\\\sum\\_\\{t=1\\}^\\{n\\}X\\_\\{t\\}\\>x\\\\right\\)\\\\leq\\\\exp\\(\\-\\\\frac\\{x^\\{2\\}\\}\\{2N\\\\sigma^\\{2\\}\\}\\)\\.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv:math/0312440v2 [math.NT] 24 Jan 2004 (https://studyres.com/doc/17290695/arxiv-math-0312440v2--math.nt--24-jan-2004)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.7 years ago; Crawled: today;     His name is spelled Markov in probability theory. ... Let n ≥ 2 be an integer; denote by En the set of real numbers which are not ...     all θ ∈ R, inequality (2.11) has an infinite number of solutions in integers p and q with q > 0 and\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nНаучни приноси на проф. доктор на науките Гено Петков (https://authors.uni-sofia.bg/AuthorPublications.aspx?id=4f3e